Treat “clients” as students and families unless actual organizations are supplied. Never fabricate endorsements, student names, photos, reviews, or client logos. Use neutral labeled placeholders during development. Include a simple content field indicating publication permission and display identifiable student media or testimonials only when permission is explicitly enabled. This field records the owner's confirmation; it is not a consent collection system.

## Supplied images and PNG-only workflow

I have an `images` folder containing the website's image assets. Place this folder inside the project's `public` directory so the assets are available under `public/images/` and referenced in the browser as `/images/filename.png`. Inspect the supplied files before choosing images for each section. If the folder is not yet available, prepare the paths and clearly labeled placeholders without inventing images.

Use **PNG format only** for supplied, edited, and generated raster image assets, including gallery thumbnails and larger gallery images. Do not convert them to JPEG, WebP, or AVIF. If supplied raster files are in another format, create PNG copies and preserve the originals separately from published assets. Do not merely rename file extensions.

You, the implementing developer, are responsible for preparing the supplied images for the website: resizing, suitable cropping, composition, transparent padding when needed, and lossless PNG optimization. Do not ask me to manually resize images for the gallery. Preserve original source files and create derived PNG variants with descriptive filenames. Do not upscale small images unnecessarily, stretch images, crop out important faces or instruments, or alter a person's appearance. Use consistent gallery frames with adjustable focal points, and show the full photograph in the lightbox when available.

Create appropriately sized PNG thumbnail and larger-view variants where useful, declare image dimensions, and use responsive image selection and lazy loading to manage PNG file sizes. Store image paths, alt text, captions, publication permission, and any editable focal-point settings in `data.js`. Keep generated image preparation utilities separate from the public content file, and document how to prepare newly added images. Apply the same PNG-only rule to raster branding and favicon assets; CSS-drawn decorative shapes and interface icons may remain code-based.

